A||||8||Lynx||Replacing clutch||||1
Z||000000||Lynx||11-01-1999||11:02 AM||||Ok i destoyed the clutch in my 85SC 4cyl. Lets just say i slammed a few gears and hit a couple of wrong gears and boom. Well what i want to know is the best way and the easiest way to replace it i have heard drop the frame with the engine and tranny suspended underneath. I have also heard you dont have to do that much work. Please help me i want to get this done as fast as possible.<P>Thanks<BR>Lynx||||reg||1
Z||000001||penetrator||11-01-1999||12:02 PM||||When i changed my clutch, the man who helped me said that it takes about 16 houres to change it in both ways. To be sure that it will be made in the right way he said the best is to drop the whole package down.<BR>The other way was to remove the left tire and suspension, but it will be very tight to work thru that way. But thats all i know about it.<BR>Magnus||||reg||7
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||11-01-1999||01:59 PM||||A few years ago I was having problems shifting into any gear.  I decided it was the clutch needing replacement.  It wasn't the clutch.  Then I thought , must be the hydraulic system, it wasn't. It was the cluch release device that the slave piston pushes, it was the old version and had cracked .  Replaced -shifting , no problem.  Now to your question of how to do the clutch.  I lossened the left front and rear motor/tranny mounts and the long bolts that go up and secure the body and cradle together.  Pulled the left rear tire, wheel and strut asembly.  and let everything drop.  I separated the tranny from the motor, after pulling the shift linkage loose and disconnecting the hydraulic clutch slave assmbly from the motor.  When first taking out two of the top (left and right) bolts connecting the tranny to motor, I inserted two (approx. 9 inch)bolts of the same thread size (with the thread only 1-2 inches) into the bolt holes.  Then I removed the rest of the bolts holding motor and trans together.  This put the weight on the long bolts and I could then slide the trans away from the motor and get into the cluch housing and replace the clutch, throwout, etc.  It was tight and there are probably more items to remove that I don't recall right now. But it worked for me.||||reg||7
Z||000003||theogre||11-01-1999||03:10 PM||||seems to me that you should be able to remove these trannys from the top. You need to support the motor either from overhead with a chain, or from under with blocking, but it shouldn't be imposible to do. I've done it with FWD cars that had about the same space to work in.<P>Stuff something in the holes or better yet drain the thing first. you usually have to rotate the tranny a bit to clear things comming up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||David||11-01-1999||05:09 PM||||Lynx<P>I replaced the clutch on my '86 4 cyl two times and both times I removed the engine from the top.  To do this, you have to remove the alternator, water pump, and AC compressor.  I have found this to be easier to do than removing the cradle, due to the fact that I do this in my limited space garage.||||reg||1
Z||000005||dogtired||11-02-1999||09:27 AM||||This is kind of one of those six of one and 1/2 dozen of the other answers. If it were me doing the clutch replacement I would go at it from underneath. Put a 4 X 4 under the jack points to raise the car, as described in the manual (haynes I think) to drop the cradle but I only take out the back 2 cradle bolts and lower the cradle as far as other things in the engine compartment will let me. I wouldn't remove tranny from car just unbolt it and work within that 2 or 3 inches. Remember that the tranny mounts are actually your rear motor mounts so support the motor. I put short 4 X 4 on top of the shock towers and a 2 X 6 across them and chained the motor to that. There is no rocket science here, it just takes time! If you remove your left axle and I did to make a little more room to work, Drain tranny first, it makes quite a mess. Good Luck!||||reg||10
Z||000006||Lynx||11-02-1999||01:44 PM||||I have more problems now than i had before. Way to many decisions on what to do. Ill i want to do is JUST the clutch. I also want to know about wht is the best clutch to use for the dollar. I can get a OEM replacement kit for $145 at a local dealer or i can buy a centerforce 1 for $135 plus the cost of a throw-out bearing. Its just the 4cyl but i can tear stuff up.||||reg||5
Z||000007||theogre||11-02-1999||04:52 PM||||for that little of a diferance the centerforce is not a bad deal. I'd do that.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||11-03-1999||06:27 PM||||Ogre is correct on this one. Centerforce clutches (especially the Dual Friction) are about the best performance clutch you can buy right now.||||reg||1
